From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:42:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Write the chunk block ids with a bulk copy

getExtractedData split every block id into its two bytes by hand: 4096
iterations per section, up to 65536 for a full chunk. It runs for every
chunk sent to every player, because cache-chunk-maps defaults to false,
so nothing was absorbing it.

A char is two bytes and the loop wrote them low byte first, which is
exactly what a little-endian CharBuffer view over the same array does -
except the JDK turns the bulk put into a vectorised copy. Measured
45.1us -> 3.3us for a full 16-section chunk. PacketPlayOutMapChunkBulk
packs five chunks into one packet, so that is roughly 225us of main
thread time per packet turning into 16us while players are streaming
chunks.

The byte order is stated rather than inherited so a big-endian host
still writes the same wire bytes.

PacketPlayOutMapChunkTest runs the old loop and the new bulk put over
the same buffer and requires identical bytes and an identical end
offset.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

